Basic relationship:
(f continous in [a, b]), F(x)=\int{a, x, f(t)dt}, x in [a, b], derivable and F'(x)=f(x)

F(x+h)-F(x)=\int{a, x, f(t)dt} + \int{x, x+h, f(t)dt} - \int{a, x, f(t)dt} = f(S)(x+h-x), F'(x)=lim{h->0, (F(x+h)-F(x))/h}=lim{S->x, f(S)=f(x)}
d/dx(\int{a, x, f(t)dt}) = lim{h->0, (\int{a, x+h, f(t)dt}-\int{a, x, f(t)dt})/h} = lim{h->0, \int{x, x+h, f(t)dt}/h} = lim{h->0, (f(c)*(x+h-x))/h} [x<c<x+h] = f(x)

Undefined values of start/end points: \int{0, 1, ln(x)dx}, \int{0, pi/2, tan(x)dx} -> lim{l->0+, \int{l, 1, ln(x)}}
Unlimited integration interval: \int{-\inf, \inf, 1/(x^2+1)}
Both: \int{1, \inf, 1/(x-1)dx}

Suppose that the function f is continuous on a < x < b, where a and b may be finite or infinite, if both limits exists, the integral is convergent, otherwise divergent
\int{a, b, f(x)dx} = lim{x->b-, F(x)} - lim{x->a+, F(x)}

The integral of the area forms the ordinate set. In a pair (x, y) of orthorectified coordinates (ortonormerade koordinater) on the plane, the first horizontal coordinate x is called the abscissa (abskissa) and the second vertical coordinate y is called the ordinate (ordinata).
Y_f = {(x, y): a <= x <= b, 0 <= y <= f(x)} -> A=m(Y_f)=\int{a, b, f(x)dx}
m(Y_(-f)) = \int{a, b, -f(x)dx} = -\int{a, b, f(x)dx} = -m(D)

f and g are continous and f(x) >= g(x) on [a, b], D: {(x, y): a <= x <= b, g(X) <= y <= f(x)}
m(Y_f\Y_g) = m(Y_f)-m(Y_g) = \int{a, b, f(x)dx}-\int{a, b, g(x)dx} =\int{a, b, (f(x)-g(x))dx}
A=m(D)-m(E) = m(Y_(f+k))-m(Y_(g+k)) = \int{a, b, (f(x)+k)dx} - \int{a, b, (g(x)+k)dx}

K{x=x(t), y=y(t), t in I}, x'(t), y'(t) continous [a, b], x'(t)>0 on (a, b), f(t)>=0 on [a, b]
A=\int{a, b, f(t)g'(t)dt}
Y_K = {(x, y): g(a)<=x<=g(b), 0<=y<=f(g^(-1)(x))}
m(Y_K)=\int{g(a), g(b), f(g^(-1)(x))dx} = [t=g^(-1)(x), x=g(t), dx/dt=g'(t), x=g(a) -> t=a, x=g(b) -> t=b] = \int{a, b, f(t)g'(t)}

Y_f={(x, y): a<=x<=b, 0<=y<=f(x)}, V=\int{\pi*y^2*dx} = \int{\pi*f^2(x)*dx}
a=x_0<x_1<…<x_(k-1)<x_k<…<x_n=b, \Dx_k = x_k-x_(k-1), d=max{k=1,2…n, \Dx_k}, f continous [x_(k-1, x_k], max M_k=f(\b_k), min m_k=f(\a_k), \a_k, \b_k in [x_(k-1), x_k]
\S{k=1, n, \pi*f^2(\a_k)\Dx_k} <= \S{k=1, n, m(D_k)} <= \S{k=1, n, pi*f^2(\b_k)\Dx_k} (lim d->0) -> V_x = \int{\pi*f^2(x)*dx}

Y_f={(x, y): a<=x<=b, 0<=y<=f(x)}, V=\int{a, b, 2\pi*xydx}=\int{a, b, 2\pi*xf(x)dx}
[x, x+\Dx] (\Dx>0) -> max: M_k = f(d_k), min: m_k=f(c_k), c_k, d_k in [x, x+\Dx], \DV(x) = V(x+\Dx)-V(x), B=\pi(x+\Dx)^2-\pi*x^2=\pi(2x+\Dx)\Dx
Bm_k <= \DV(x) <= BM_k -> lim{x->0, \pi(2x+\Dx)f(c_k) <= \DV(x)/\Dx <= \pi(2x+\Dx)f(d_k)} -> \pi*2x*f(x) <= dV/dx <= \pi*2x*f(x)
